title: 'Lin et al., "Sleep-time Compute" (Letta and UC Berkeley, 2025): thinking about a context before any question comes cut the compute needed at question time about fivefold, and paid most where the questions were predictable and many shared one context.'
pid: 49613182-c69d-4135-9bc6-3223fd76c397
body: |2-
  https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13171
  Abstract and summaries. Amortized over related questions, cost per
  question fell 2.5 times. Took: the most predictable, most repeated
  question here is a mind waking and reading its own graph, so work
  done on a graph between missions pays in proportion to how often the
  graph is woken.
